WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.518
Here's a function with just one line in its body.

00:00:03.518 --> 00:00:05.620
It takes a shape, and prints it.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.370
We call it twice: once with a circle, and once with a rectangle.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.304
The same line of code runs both times.

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:18.880
So why does it print a radius the first time, and a width and a height the second?

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:24.347
This is Lecture 8, part 3: polymorphism and method lookup.

00:00:24.697 --> 00:00:28.124
We'll write functions that work for every kind of shape,

00:00:28.124 --> 00:00:32.163
and then learn the 1 rule Python uses to decide which method runs.

00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:36.937
With that rule, you can answer any question of the form: what does this print?

00:00:37.734 --> 00:00:42.227
Every circle is a geometric object, and so is every rectangle.

00:00:42.227 --> 00:00:47.734
So a function written for a GeometricObject parameter works for any of them.

00:00:48.084 --> 00:00:50.836
Display object prints its argument.

00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:53.196
Is same area compares 2 areas.

00:00:53.196 --> 00:00:59.644
Main makes a circle of radius 4 and a rectangle that's 1 by 3, and passes them in.

00:00:59.994 --> 00:01:02.118
The 1st line shows the radius.

00:01:02.118 --> 00:01:04.879
The 2nd shows the width and the height.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:07.074
And the areas are not the same.

00:01:07.424 --> 00:01:11.562
This is polymorphism, which is Greek for many forms.

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:17.292
An object of a subclass can be used wherever its superclass is expected,

00:01:17.292 --> 00:01:22.704
and the code that uses it doesn't need to know which subclass it is.

00:01:23.304 --> 00:01:28.786
And which dunder str runs isn't decided when you write the function.

00:01:28.786 --> 00:01:31.769
It's decided each time the line runs,

00:01:31.769 --> 00:01:36.525
by the class of the object that g refers to at that moment.

00:01:36.525 --> 00:01:39.024
This is called dynamic binding.

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:42.055
Here's the pattern behind it.

00:01:42.055 --> 00:01:43.921
Call it: ask the object.

00:01:44.271 --> 00:01:49.955
Write a function in terms of methods that every object of the superclass has,

00:01:49.955 --> 00:01:50.989
like get area.

00:01:50.989 --> 00:01:53.351
Let each subclass override them.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:57.842
Total area adds up the area of every shape in a list.

00:01:57.842 --> 00:02:01.123
It never checks what kind of shape it has.

00:02:01.123 --> 00:02:03.701
Each shape uses its own get area.

00:02:04.051 --> 00:02:06.451
3 rectangles give 36.

00:02:06.451 --> 00:02:11.251
A circle and a rectangle give about 11.14.

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:17.290
And if someone writes a triangle class next year, total area will work for it too,

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:19.441
without changing a single line.

00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:23.832
So how does Python find the right method?

00:02:23.832 --> 00:02:28.834
Here's the rule, for any attribute: a method, or a field.

00:02:29.184 --> 00:02:34.904
Step 1: look among the object's own attributes, the ones dunder init put on it.

00:02:35.254 --> 00:02:39.134
Step 2: otherwise, look in the object's class.

00:02:39.484 --> 00:02:43.178
Step 3: otherwise, look in that class's superclass,

00:02:43.178 --> 00:02:46.364
then its superclass, and so on up the chain.

00:02:46.714 --> 00:02:49.610
Step 4: the chain always ends at object.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:54.314
If the name isn't there either, Python raises an attribute error.

00:02:54.664 --> 00:02:57.488
The 1st match wins, and the search stops.

00:02:57.488 --> 00:03:01.344
This search order is called the method resolution order.

00:03:02.134 --> 00:03:03.851
Let's use the rule.

00:03:03.851 --> 00:03:06.380
Here's a chain of 4 classes.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:11.348
C1 sets f to 1, and has an output method that prints f.

00:03:11.348 --> 00:03:15.774
C2 extends C1, sets f to 2, and overrides output.

00:03:16.124 --> 00:03:20.020
C3 extends C2, and C4 extends C3.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.797
Each of them sets f, to 3 and 4.

00:03:23.797 --> 00:03:28.164
But neither one has an output method.

00:03:28.514 --> 00:03:31.589
We make a C4 object, called a.

00:03:31.589 --> 00:03:34.459
Pause the video and predict.

00:03:34.459 --> 00:03:37.022
What does print a.f show?

00:03:37.022 --> 00:03:39.994
And what does a.output print?

00:03:40.544 --> 00:03:46.518
Making a C4 looks for dunder init, and finds it in C4 straight away.

00:03:46.518 --> 00:03:49.504
So only that one runs, and f is 4.

00:03:49.854 --> 00:03:51.305
Now a.output.

00:03:51.305 --> 00:03:55.100
Look in the object: it only has f.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.556
Look in C4: no output.

00:03:57.556 --> 00:03:59.454
In C3: no output.

00:03:59.454 --> 00:04:01.574
In C2: found. Stop.

00:04:01.924 --> 00:04:06.039
So C2's output runs, with self being a.

00:04:06.039 --> 00:04:09.099
It prints: in C2, the f is 4.

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:15.324
The method came from C2, but the data came from the object.

00:04:15.924 --> 00:04:21.173
And you can ask Python for the order it searched.

00:04:21.173 --> 00:04:25.564
For C4, it's C4, C3, C2, C1, then object.

00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:30.214
Now the most common wrong idea about method lookup.

00:04:30.564 --> 00:04:37.262
Many students think the method that runs is the one in the class where the call is written.

00:04:37.262 --> 00:04:37.924
It isn't.

00:04:38.524 --> 00:04:39.682
Look at this.

00:04:39.682 --> 00:04:43.778
Student has a dunder str that returns student,

00:04:43.778 --> 00:04:48.675
and a print student method that prints self.dunder str.

00:04:48.675 --> 00:04:54.284
GraduateStudent extends Student, and overrides only dunder str.

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:58.261
For a plain Student, print student prints: student.

00:04:58.261 --> 00:04:59.114
No surprise.

00:04:59.464 --> 00:05:02.994
Now call print student on b, a GraduateStudent.

00:05:02.994 --> 00:05:06.975
Python doesn't find print student in GraduateStudent,

00:05:06.975 --> 00:05:09.904
so it goes up, and finds it in Student.

00:05:10.254 --> 00:05:12.866
Inside it, self is still b.

00:05:12.866 --> 00:05:18.865
So self.dunder str starts the search again, at the class of b,

00:05:18.865 --> 00:05:21.283
which is GraduateStudent.

00:05:21.283 --> 00:05:26.894
It finds the override there, and prints: graduate student.

00:05:27.494 --> 00:05:31.788
A method written in the superclass called a method of the subclass.

00:05:31.788 --> 00:05:33.774
That's dynamic binding at work.

00:05:34.568 --> 00:05:35.253
1 twist.

00:05:35.253 --> 00:05:38.768
These 2 programs differ in a single name.

00:05:39.118 --> 00:05:42.101
On the left, the method is called get info.

00:05:42.101 --> 00:05:45.918
On the right, it's private, with 2 leading underscores.

00:05:46.268 --> 00:05:48.994
The left one prints person, then student.

00:05:48.994 --> 00:05:51.588
That's dynamic binding, just as before.

00:05:51.938 --> 00:05:56.738
But the right one prints person, twice. Why?

00:05:57.288 --> 00:06:02.267
Name mangling happens when the code is written, not when it runs.

00:06:02.267 --> 00:06:06.404
Inside Person, the call is renamed with Person's name.

00:06:06.404 --> 00:06:10.848
Inside Student, the method is renamed with Student's name.

00:06:11.198 --> 00:06:16.663
The 2 names are different, so Student's method doesn't override Person's.

00:06:16.663 --> 00:06:19.358
Private methods can't be overridden.

00:06:20.135 --> 00:06:21.655
Let's sum up.

00:06:22.005 --> 00:06:25.814
Polymorphism: 1 function works for every subclass.

00:06:25.814 --> 00:06:30.005
Ask the object, and let each class override the method.

00:06:30.355 --> 00:06:36.496
Method lookup: start at the object, then its class, then up the chain to object.

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:37.955
The 1st match wins.

00:06:38.305 --> 00:06:43.954
Inside a method, a call on self starts again at the object's own class.

00:06:43.954 --> 00:06:45.785
That's dynamic binding.

00:06:46.135 --> 00:06:47.570
Here's 1 to try.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.351
Animal has speak and introduce.

00:06:50.351 --> 00:06:52.145
Dog overrides speak.

00:06:52.145 --> 00:06:56.003
Puppy overrides introduce, and calls super.

00:06:56.003 --> 00:07:01.655
Trace what each one's introduce returns, using the lookup rule.

00:07:02.205 --> 00:07:05.965
In part 4, we'll meet the is instance function,

00:07:05.965 --> 00:07:09.165
and classes with more than 1 superclass.

